<!--
  clause 8.7.1 HDR ColorSpace Profile declaring VideoFullRangeFlag = 0.

  This fixture is one half of a PAIR. `HdrFullRangeFlag.xml` and
  `HdrNarrowRangeFlag.xml` are identical in every byte except the
  `VideoFullRangeFlag` field of the cicpTag, which is narrow range (0) here.

  WHAT THE PAIR IS FOR - register item IMPL-02.  Nothing in this implementation
  reads `VideoFullRangeFlag`.  `icGetHdrProfileInfo()` resolves it into
  `icHdrProfileInfo::bVideoFullRange` and no transform consults it, which the
  struct comment states plainly: "a narrow-range HDR ColorSpace Profile is legal and
  nothing here acts on it".

  So these two profiles currently render IDENTICALLY.  That is the point.  The
  pair is not a passing test - it is the gap made visible and pinned, so that
  whichever way IMPL-02 is resolved, the resolution has a fixture that must
  change.  A build that starts honouring the flag makes them differ; a ruling
  that narrow range is not permitted in a clause 8.7.1 profile makes the narrow
  half a validation negative.  Either outcome moves this pair.

  WHY IT MATTERS HERE AND NOT FOR THE v5 FIXTURES.  Four of the ten BT.2100
  fixtures in this directory are narrow-range and are correct, because a v5
  multiProcessElement pipeline carries the range expansion as an explicit
  curve: `BT2100PQNarrowDisplay.xml` opens with a `SingleSampledCurve` running
  0,0625152 to 0,9181929 - the 10-bit studio-swing pair 64/1023 and 940/1023.
  The transform is self-contained there and the CICP field is descriptive.

  A clause 8.7.1 HDR ColorSpace Profile has no such curve.  Its transfer is IMPLIED by
  `cicpTag.TransferCharacteristics` and applied directly, so if the flag says
  narrow and nothing expands first, the EOTF is evaluated on unexpanded code
  values - a lifted black and a clipped white, silently.

  Both halves are CONFORMING HDR ColorSpace Profiles - clause 8.7.1 never mentions the
  field, so neither violates anything.  They no longer validate identically,
  though: since 2026-09-08 `CheckHdrProfile()` emits a WARNING on the narrow
  half saying this implementation does not expand narrow-range values.  That
  warning is about the implementation, not the file, and it is the first thing
  in the tree that makes the flag observable at all.  The full half stays
  clean, which is what proves the diagnostic keys on this one field.

  Whether clause 8.7.1 INTENDS to permit narrow range remains the open question
  IMPL-02 records; the diagnostic reports our limitation, it does not answer
  the spec question.
-->
